# MicroOS installation input

The input release and documentation choices are in `../../parts/microos-release.tsv` and `../../parts/microos-choices.md`. The image is the DVD installer, not a preinstalled disk image. The account and SSH key are laboratory instrument access.

Execute on the laboratory host, with this directory copied to `C1/microos/` beneath the laboratory root:

1. Run `download.sh` and `verify.sh` to verify the published SHA256 and detached signature with the official project key. Verification evidence: `../../raw/microos/verification.txt`. Key URL: https://download.opensuse.org/tumbleweed/repo/oss/repodata/repomd.xml.key ; verified fingerprint `AD485664E901B867051AB15F35A2F86E29B700A4`.
2. Run `prepare.sh`. This extracts only the published installer kernel/initrd, prepares the fixed AutoYaST input, creates the blank disk/UEFI variables, and inserts `keys/lab.pub` into the Combustion input. It does not read a check installation.
3. Start `install.sh` as a durable user service. It takes one available slot, suppresses all installer and guest output, installs with defaults, and reboots once. The first role has no overrides relative to the product defaults. `autoinst.xml` explicitly transcribes the product software defaults (because AutoYaST does not inherit them automatically), retains the automatic partition proposal, and adds the Combustion instrument package and first-boot preparation.
4. Boot the installed disk with the fixed Combustion blob. Only SSH login and fixed exit 37, including through sudo, are tested. Each command is bounded. Only exit statuses are retained in `status.log`; there is no guest output log.
5. On success the script requests shutdown. Move the stopped disk to `C1/base/microos.qcow2`, keep it read-only, copy the UEFI variables to `install/microos/OVMF_VARS.fd`, and register metadata. Verify the shared runner on a disposable overlay using only fixed exit statuses.

The first attempted extraction also requested `/control.xml` from the ISO; this optional documentation extraction returned status 5 because that path was absent. Kernel/initrd extraction succeeded. The final recipe omits the unavailable documentation path; authoritative role documentation is pinned to the upstream source commit instead. No guest had been booted at that point.

Expected instrument result fixed before the installation: SSH returns status 37 for `exit 37` and for `sudo -n sh -c 'exit 37'`; orderly shutdown exits the emulator. Readiness requires those statuses and a successful shared-runner check. A failed or timed-out login gives no information about packages, services, files, accounts, or other check contents; it is only a laboratory failure.

Attempt 1: installer emulator exited 0 after 158 seconds. The following installed-disk boot did not provide SSH login in the first four minutes (only exit 255 was retained). It was stopped without inspecting any guest contents. Its three inputs are preserved in `attempt-1/`; its disposable disk was deleted.

Attempt 2: add the documented `combustion.firstboot` kernel parameter explicitly, create the access account's own primary group, and explicitly include the documented firstboot/Combustion initramfs modules. Combustion removes that access parameter from `/etc/kernel/cmdline` when finished; the transaction's boot entry generation applies the resulting command line. Sources: https://github.com/openSUSE/combustion/blob/master/README.md and https://github.com/openSUSE/sdbootutil/blob/main/sdbootutil . The boot parameter location is documented by https://doc.opensuse.org/projects/autoyast/index.html . A loopback-bound host callback accepts only named command exit statuses, enabling diagnosis of installation/access failures without retaining any command output. `status-server.py` rejects other paths and records no HTTP request headers or addresses. This is instrument installation, not a check probe.

Attempt 2: installer emulator exited 0 after 214 seconds; neither the optional command-status callbacks nor SSH access became available during four minutes of installed-disk boot. Its inputs are preserved in `attempt-2/`; its disposable disk was stopped and deleted. This does not establish whether the installation completed.

Attempt 3 changes only delivery of the same fixed installation profile: the official documented `autoyast=http://...` path replaces the appended-initrd path. The host server serves only the fixed `autoinst.xml` and the status endpoint. A pre-install hook also returns its exit status. The installer kernel/initrd remain the verified ISO's original files. This permits distinguishing profile delivery from later installation stages using only instrument statuses.

Attempt 3: profile delivery and the pre-install hook returned success; the emulator exited 0 after 205 seconds. No later callback or SSH login was obtained during more than three minutes of installed-disk boot. The disk was stopped and deleted; inputs are in `attempt-3/`.

Attempt 4 explicitly requires the `combustion` package solely for first-boot instrument access. The public default-pattern source explicitly lists `sudo`, `openssh`, and `curl`, but not `combustion`: https://api.opensuse.org/public/source/openSUSE:Factory/patterns-microos/patterns-microos.spec (saved under `raw/microos/`). This is documentation about the distribution recipe, not an observation of any check machine. No default pattern is removed or replaced. A second serial channel is now used only for fixed `C1STATUS:<command>:<exit>` records from the supplied scripts. The host parser discards every other byte without retaining or exposing it. The first serial console remains discarded. This allows command-exit diagnosis even if guest networking is unavailable.

Attempt 4 was stopped before acceptance after a public-source review identified a concrete recipe defect: in AutoYaST mode, YaST does not append the interactive installer’s default/optional patterns. Earlier assumptions that an absent patterns list preserves those defaults were incorrect. No guest inspection established any installed content, and those attempts are not accepted. Inputs remain in `attempt-4/`; disk deleted.

Attempt 5 transcribes the pinned control file’s four mandatory default patterns and the optional `32bit` pattern (regex-if-available), sets the documented MicroOS recommended-package default to false, and retains only the Combustion access addition. Source excerpts: `raw/microos/autoyast-defaults-source.txt`. Status-channel writes are best-effort so a status channel cannot itself fail installation.

Attempt 5 was stopped after independent static review found the unattended license boolean inverted. The official AutoYaST documentation says, under `confirm_base_product_license`, “The user needs to accept this license.” Setting it to true displays the EULA; its unattended default is false. Source: https://doc.opensuse.org/documentation/leap/autoyast/html/book-autoyast/cha-configuration-installation-options.html . The profile otherwise passed the official MicroOS XML schema in static review. Inputs retained in `attempt-5/`; disk stopped and deleted. Attempt 6 changes only that boolean to false. No guest output was read to find this defect.

The concrete public-key-bearing first-boot input is `combustion`. The reusable template is `combustion.template`; the private laboratory key is never published. The original-image kernel/initrd and HTTP profile are used by the final recipe; the appended initrd generated by `prepare.sh` is retained only for historical compatibility and is not booted.

Final revisit began 2026-11-23T06:55:05Z after the resistance boundary and work on the other systems. Public AutoYaST `inst_autosetup` source confirms that `confirm_base_product_license=false` skips its interactive license loop (`raw/microos/autosetup-review.txt`). No second concrete static defect was established. The existing corrected attempt is preserved through its previously fixed installer deadline; no speculative guest-state diagnosis is made from missing callbacks.

Final result: attempt 6 reached its fixed 2400-second installer deadline at 2026-11-23T07:14:22Z with exit 124. Profile delivery and the pre-install hook were successful, but no later-stage or login success was recorded. With all installer/guest output discarded, the internal failing step cannot be identified. No clean installation or shared-runner success is claimed. The stopped partial disk, mutable firmware, extracted boot material, temporary keyring, and FIFOs were deleted; exact inputs and numeric statuses remain.
